776733398438, 0.0013570785522460938, 0.046295166015625, -0.0229644775390625, 0.0233154296875, -0.0004553794860839844, 0.00860595703125, 0.0202484130859375, 0.01910400390625, 0.041595458984375, -0.005100250244140625, 0.04998779296875, 0.016876220703125, 0.037750244140625, -0.00965118408203125, 0.0001577138900756836, -0.007045745849609375, -0.01995849609375, 0.0005636215209960938, -0.0066986083984375, -0.0254364013671875, 0.0135345458984375, 0.050872802734375, -0.00928497314453125, -0.0185089111328125, -0.0299224853515625, 0.026275634765625, -0.00548553466796875, 0.0003154277801513672, 0.05224609375, 0.01922607421875, -0.0178680419921875, -0.03790283203125, 0.00164031982421875, 0.00010097026824951172, 0.004520416259765625, -0.03582763671875, 0.01430511474609375, -0.0095977783203125, -0.0023956298828125, -0.01136016845703125, -0.007537841796875, 0.0241546630859375, 0.01200103759765625, -0.0019245147705078125, -0.0156097412109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\\section*{Introduction}\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, limiting the synergy between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n\\section*{Methodology}\n\nWe introduce a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}, a framework that systematically organizes and curates data to support the progressive acquisition of generative capabilities. This infrastructure integrates \\textbf{capability-aligned curriculum scheduling}, which sequences data exposure based on the dependencies among tasks, facilitating more effective model training.\n\nThe framework consists of three specialized yet interoperable data engines:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Text-Image Grounding Engine}: Constructs supervision for aligning textual descriptions with images, enabling robust text-to-image (T2I) generation.\n    \\item \\textbf{Inter-Image Transformation Engine}: Builds paired data for image editing and transformation tasks, supporting capabilities such as inpainting, style transfer, and object manipulation.\n    \\item \\textbf{Image-Knowledge Association Engine}: Associates images with structured knowledge entities, enhancing the model's ability to ground visual content in external knowledge.\n\\end{itemize}\nCaption experts further align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, ensuring consistency and interoperability.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum jointly evolves task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution along the dependency order of capability acquisition. This curriculum is guided by \\textbf{capability-aware evaluation}, which employs targeted retrieval, expert construction, and gap-aware resampling to iteratively refine the data. Evaluation metrics include retrieval accuracy, coverage of visual concepts, and performance on downstream generative tasks.\n\n\\section*{Results}\n\nAt scale, our infrastructure curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. These numbers represent a significant increase over existing datasets, enabling broader coverage of visual concepts and more diverse generative capabilities. For comparison, previous large-scale datasets such as LAION-400M focus primarily on T2I pairs, while our framework extends to editing and knowledge association at unprecedented scale.\n\nWe train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters, respectively. Quantitative evaluation on CPI-Bench demonstrates improvements in visual coverage and generative versatility. For example, our models achieve a 7\\% higher retrieval accuracy and a 12\\% increase in concept diversity compared to baselines. Qualitative evaluations across diverse text-to-image and editing scenarios further illustrate effective transfer and rendering fidelity, as shown in Figure~1.\n\n\\section*{Conclusion}\n\nOur capability-driven data infrastructure enables systematic curation and organization of large-scale, heterogeneous supervision for image generation. By aligning data construction and curriculum scheduling with generative capability dependencies, we achieve broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across tasks. This framework advances the state of the art in multimodal generative modeling and provides a scalable foundation for future research."}}